{:edb/db-before-t 1705 :edb/db-after-t 1706 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535018 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:44:14.690Z" 13194139535018 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327103 13194139535018 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327110 13194139535018 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327089 13194139535018 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327096 13194139535018 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327117 13194139535018 true] [17592186134142 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535018 true] [17592186134142 1100 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖ = 5$ and $‖2u + 3v‖ = 17$. If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖$." 13194139535018 false] [17592186134142 1100 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖=5$ and $‖2u+3v‖=17.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖.$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186134142 1145 "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $2u$ is orthogonal to $3v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =‖2u{‖}^{2}+‖3v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =(2‖u‖)^{2}+(3‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖2u+3v{‖}^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (17)^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9(5)^{2} \\\\ 289 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 289 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+225 \\\\ 64 & =4‖u{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖u‖=4.$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186134189 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535018 true] [17592186134189 1100 "Given that $‖4u + v‖ = 22$ and $‖v‖ = 2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$." 13194139535018 false] [17592186134189 1100 "Given that $‖4u+v‖=22$ and $‖v‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186134189 1145 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le ‖4u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le 4‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 22 & \\le 4‖u‖+2 \\\\ 20 & \\le 4‖u‖ \\\\ 5 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $5.$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327090 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1089 "q-140462" 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1100 "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}=204$ and $‖u‖=4$ and $‖w‖=7$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1113 #uuid "4b890941-b213-56f5-bd82-e5a5ff2f26e9" 13194139535018 true] [17592186327089 1145 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16‖v{‖}^{2}+3‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+3(‖v{‖}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =19‖v{‖}^{2}+147.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}204 & \\le 19‖v‖+147 \\\\ 57 & \\le 19‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 3 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $3\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{3}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327091 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327092 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327093 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327094 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327095 13194139535018 true] [17592186327090 1040 #edb/re